## Python Packaging

A Python project can be turned into an installable package.

Modern Python projects commonly use:

pyproject.toml

A package can contain:

* Source code
* Metadata
* Dependencies
* Tests
* Documentation
* Configuration

Packages can be distributed through package repositories such as PyPI.

---

## Wheels

A wheel is a built Python distribution format.

Example:

package_name-1.0.0-py3-none-any.whl

Wheels make installation easier because Python does not necessarily have to build the package from source.

Some wheels contain compiled native code.

---

## Source Distributions

A source distribution contains the source required to build/install a package.

Commonly:

.tar.gz

Python packaging can therefore involve:

Source code
     ↓
Build system
     ↓
Wheel / source distribution
     ↓
Package repository
     ↓
pip

---

## Python Enhancement Proposals

PEPs are Python Enhancement Proposals.

They describe proposed changes, standards, or guidance relating to Python.

Examples include major language features and conventions.

You should learn to read PEPs because they explain:

* Why features were introduced
* What problems they solve
* How they should be used
* What design decisions were made

Important idea:

> Advanced Python developers don't just learn features. They understand why the language was designed that way.

---

## Python Language Design

At the highest level, Python is designed around principles such as:

* Readability
* Simplicity
* Explicitness
* Consistency
* Practicality

Python's design philosophy can be explored through:

import this

This displays the Zen of Python.

Important principles include:

Readability counts.

Simple is better than complex.

Explicit is better than implicit.

Errors should never pass silently.

There should be one obvious way to do it.

These principles influence how Python develops.

---

## Alternative Python Implementations

CPython is not the only Python implementation.

### CPython

The standard/default implementation.

Written primarily in C.

### PyPy

Uses a JIT compiler and can provide significant performance improvements for some workloads.

### MicroPython

Designed for constrained hardware and microcontrollers.

### GraalPy

Python implementation built on GraalVM.

The important lesson is:

> Python is a language specification/ecosystem, while CPython is an implementation.

---

## The Global Interpreter Lock

CPython has historically used the Global Interpreter Lock, commonly called the GIL.

The GIL affects how Python threads execute Python code.

It is particularly important for CPU-bound workloads.

The important concepts are:

* Threads
* Processes
* CPU-bound work
* I/O-bound work
* Parallelism
* Concurrency
* GIL

Modern Python development also requires understanding the newer free-threaded/no-GIL work in Python, rather than assuming the historical GIL model is the only possible model.

---

## C Extensions

Python can interact with native C code.

This is important because Python itself is implemented largely in C through CPython.

C extensions can be used when:

* Performance is critical
* Existing C libraries need to be used
* Low-level functionality is required

Technologies include:

* CPython C API
* Cython
* `ctypes`
* `cffi`

Many high-performance Python libraries rely on native code underneath.

Examples include parts of:

* NumPy
* SciPy
* PyTorch

