## What Python Mastery Actually Means

Being a Python master does NOT mean:

* Memorising every standard library function
* Knowing every obscure syntax feature
* Using advanced features everywhere
* Writing unnecessarily clever code
* Knowing every framework

Instead, mastery means you can:

* Read unfamiliar Python
* Design large Python systems
* Debug difficult problems
* Understand memory behaviour
* Understand object behaviour
* Use the standard library effectively
* Write maintainable code
* Optimise when necessary
* Understand concurrency
* Understand Python internals
* Read CPython source
* Understand PEPs
* Build Python packages
* Contribute to open source
* Explain why Python behaves the way it does

The most important progression is:

Beginner
"I can write Python."

        ↓

Intermediate
"I can solve problems with Python."

        ↓

Advanced
"I understand Python's advanced features."

        ↓

Expert
"I understand why Python behaves the way it does."

        ↓

Master
"I understand the language, its implementation,
its ecosystem, and how to extend or change it."

